  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "One Grung Above",
              "href": "https://www.dmsguild.com/product/223738/One-Grung-Above-5e?affiliate_id=389627"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " ("
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "affiliate link",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/about/faq#what-are-affiliate-links-and-why-do-you-use-them"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ") was published in 2017 as part of Wizards of the Coasts annual fundraising drive for Extra Life. It’s a 4-page PDF devoted almost entirely to stat blocks for characters used in a streamed game session, and finishes with a half-page entry with traits for the Grung as a playable race."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Thematically, grung are halfling-sized humanoid frog people with a rigid\n\tcaste system based on the color of their skin. Grung can change their skin\n\tcolor by being subjected to a ritual, but they need to perform “great deeds”\n\tto be invited to do so. Grung also enjoy taking sentient creatures as slaves\n\tand keeping those slaves in a perpetual poisoned state. So between a caste\n\tsystem based on skin color, slavery, and war crimes grung are generally just\n\tawful people. But sometimes playing an exception to a race’s published culture\n\tcan be fun, and sometimes you want to play villains."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the Grung is a mess. Many traits are copied entirely from the\n\tmonster entries for the Grung, and those traits were absolutely not written\n\tfor use as a playable race. While they work fine for a self-contained one-shot\n\tgame, they have some issues in a long-form campaign. In general, the race is\n\texceptionally powerful."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dex/Con is a great combo for any martial class except the Barbarian.\n\tPerception is the most important skill in the game and you get proficiency for\n\tfree. You get a climb speed which can trivialize many obstacles, especially\n\twhen combined with the Grung’s ability to long jump 25 feet, allowing you to\n\tbypass obstacles like pits or difficult terrain with no effort. Poison\n\tImmunity protects from a very common damage type which is a problem at all\n\tlevels. Poisonous Skin offers a small damage boost, and while the DC is fixed\n\tat 12 and it’s only 2d4 damage, the target is Poisoned for 1 minute (though\n\tthey do get additional saves), severely deblitating them. Even though the DC\n\tis low, even high-level creatures can still fail the save (even if it’s only\n\ton a natural 1), and as players gain additional attacks the chances of foes\n\tbeing poisoned multiply."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-5",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Grung would need a lot of work to be considered balanced enough to make\n\tit to publication. As some quick fixes, I recommend reworking Poisonous Skin\n\tto use a DC equal to 8 + Proficiency Bonus + Constitution Modifier, to work\n\tonce per short, once per or long rest, or potentially PB/long rest like many\n\tracial features as of Mordenkainen’s Tome of Foes being released. Adjust the\n\tdamage to increase by 2d4 at 5th, 11th, and 17th levels to match cantrip\n\tdamage increases in order to keep the damage relevant rather than relying\n\tentirely on the Poisoned condition. That makes it an occasional tactical\n\toption similar to the Dragonborn’s breath weapon rather than a persistent\n\tmechanical annoyance. You might also weaken Standing Leap to some degree,\n\tpossibly by duplicating the Satyr’s Mirthful Leaps trait or the Harengon’s\n\tRabbit Hop trait."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
